UPDATE courses SET
    course_description = '<h4>Degree overview</h4><p>Make a positive difference in the world</p><p>Development, poverty, inequality , political instability, and environmental sustainability are some of the most important issues in the world today. This program provides the opportunity to study the causes and consequences of unequal development. This program is the only development studies program in Australia to include economics and Aboriginal studies as core courses.</p> <p>Students focus on key global issues in local, national and international contexts. You could find yourself employed for the Commonwealth Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade, helping developing countries address their internal constraints to trade. Or locally, you could work with an Indigenous community working to improve environmental sustainability.</p><p>No matter your focus, when you study Development Studies, you can go on to make a positive difference.</p> <p> <strong> World top 150 ? </strong> for Geography </p> <p> <strong> 88% of students ? </strong> were happy with facilities and resources – above the national average </p> <h4>Career opportunities</h4><p>Skills, abilities and knowledge learnt in the Bachelor of Development Studies are highly sought after by a broad range of employers, including international aid organisations, consulting firms and local, state and federal government departments.</p> <p>Not everyone uses their degree in the same way and the transferable skills gained through university study may allow graduates to pursue a range of careers that might not be directly linked to their study. Students may work in federal, state &amp; local government, NGOs, consultancy or private business.</p><p><strong>Professional outcomes:</strong></p><ul><li>International Aid/Development</li><li>Urban and Regional Planning</li><li>Community Development</li><li>Environmental Management</li><li>Advocacy and Policy Development Work</li><li>Research</li></ul> <p>82.9% employed within four months of graduating.</p> <p>Graduate Outcomes Survey 2022-2024, Undergraduate Humanities, Culture and Social Sciences.</p> <h4>Course overview</h4><p>You will have the opportunity to choose from one of four majors:</p><ul><li><strong>Cultures and Citizenship</strong> – courses in this major will prepare you for fields related to Indigenous affairs, immigration and citizenship or community development.</li><li><strong>Environmental Sustainability</strong> – as part of this major you will study courses to develop your analytical skills. You will also learn how to evaluate the impact of development on the environment and the community.</li><li><strong>Urban and Regional Development</strong> – studying courses in this major will open up employment opportunities in the fields of planning, regional or community development.</li><li><strong>Geopolitics and Economic Development </strong>– by completing this major you could go on to work in fields related to economic policy and analysis.</li></ul><p>You will study real-world issues such as globalisation, environmental change, sustainable development, refugee and asylum seeker migration, and social justice. Our interdisciplinary approach means that in addition to your major you are exposed to a wide range of topics including:</p><ul><li>Aboriginal studies</li><li>Economics</li><li>Geography</li><li>International Relations</li><li>Sociology</li><li>Environmental studies</li></ul> <p>Core</p><p>Majors</p><p>Electives</p> <p><a href="#">Core</a></p><p><strong>Complete the following core courses to fulfil the requirements of this program.</strong></p><ul><li><strong>ABOR1110</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ABOR1110">Blak Australia</a></li><li><strong>ECON1111</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ECON1111">Economics for Business Decisions</a></li><li><strong>ENVS1003</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ENVS1003">Environmental Values and Ethics</a></li><li><strong>ENVS3008</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ENVS3008">Work Integrated Learning in the Environmental and Development Sectors</a></li><li><strong>GEOG1030</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/GEOG1030">Global Poverty and Development</a></li><li><strong>GEOG2130</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/GEOG2130">Geographies of Development</a></li><li><strong>GEOG3300</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/GEOG3300">Rethinking Development</a></li><li><strong>SOCA1010</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/SOCA1010">Society and Culture: an Introduction to Sociology</a></li></ul><p>These are courses currently available in <em>"Core"</em>, there may be additional courses available in future terms. For full requirements and course availability always refer to the <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/program/2026/11368">handbook</a>.</p><p><a href="#">1000 Level Course List</a></p><p><strong>Complete 20 units from the following course list</strong></p><ul><li><strong>ABOR1370</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ABOR1370">Community Engagement: Respect and Reciprocity</a></li><li><strong>ABOR1390</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ABOR1390">Blak Rights and the Fight for Justice</a></li><li><strong>ABOR1420</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ABOR1420">Yarning, Media and Futures</a></li><li><strong>ENVS1004</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ENVS1004">Social Development and the Environment</a></li><li><strong>GEOG1020</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/GEOG1020">Introduction to Human Geography</a></li><li><strong>SOCA1020</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/SOCA1020">What is Anthropology</a></li><li><strong>THHE1001</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/THHE1001">Introduction to Global Tourism</a></li><li><strong>THHE1002</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/THHE1002">Sustainable Tourism Systems</a></li></ul><p>These are courses currently available in <em>"1000 Level Course List"</em>, there may be additional courses available in future terms. For full requirements and course availability always refer to the <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/program/2026/11368">handbook</a>.</p><p><a href="#">2000 Level Course List</a></p><p><strong>Complete 20 units from the following course list.</strong></p><ul><li><strong>ABOR2022</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ABOR2022">Aboriginal Cultural Practices</a></li><li><strong>ABOR2244</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ABOR2244">Sovereignty and Reconciliation</a></li><li><strong>ENVS2002</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ENVS2002">Environmental Legislation and Planning</a></li><li><strong>ENVS2008</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ENVS2008">The Sustainable Society</a></li><li><strong>GEOG2080</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/GEOG2080">Cities and Regions</a></li><li><strong>IREL2001</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/IREL2001">International Policy, Politics and the Economy</a></li><li><strong>SOCA2400</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/SOCA2400">Future Societies: Beyond Capitalist Globalisation</a></li><li><strong>SOCS2400</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/SOCS2400">Surveying the Social: Applied Quantitative Research</a></li></ul><p>These are courses currently available in <em>"2000 Level Course List"</em>, there may be additional courses available in future terms. For full requirements and course availability always refer to the <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/program/2026/11368">handbook</a>.</p><p><a href="#">Cultures and Citizenship</a></p><p><strong>Compulsory </strong>Complete the following compulsory course.</p><ul><li><strong>ABOR3024</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ABOR3024">Blak Advocacy: Empowerment and Solidarity</a></li></ul><p><strong>Course List </strong>Complete 70 units including a minimum of 30 units at 3000 level.</p><ul><li><strong>ABOR1370</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ABOR1370">Community Engagement: Respect and Reciprocity</a></li><li><strong>ABOR2022</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ABOR2022">Aboriginal Cultural Practices</a></li><li><strong>ABOR2244</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ABOR2244">Sovereignty and Reconciliation</a></li><li><strong>ABOR3251</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ABOR3251">Global Indigenous Comparative Studies</a></li><li><strong>ABOR3380</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ABOR3380">Global Indigenous Human Rights</a></li><li><strong>GEOG3090</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/GEOG3090">Society and Space</a></li><li><strong>IREL2002</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/IREL2002">Business and the Politics of Globalisation</a></li><li><strong>IREL3004</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/IREL3004">Indigenising International Politics, Policy and the Economy</a></li><li><strong>SCIE3600</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/SCIE3600">International Study Experience</a></li><li><strong>SOCA1020</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/SOCA1020">What is Anthropology</a></li><li><strong>SOCA3220</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/SOCA3220">Youth Culture and Risk</a></li><li><strong>SOCA3666</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/SOCA3666">Consumption, Technology and Everyday Life</a></li><li><strong>SOCA3780</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/SOCA3780">Leading Social Change: Applying Critical Theories in Critical Times</a></li><li><strong>SOCA3790</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/SOCA3790">Mobilities, Migration and the Environment</a></li><li><strong>SOCA3850</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/SOCA3850">Rage Against the Machine: Indigenous Activism, Ways of Life and the Future</a></li><li><strong>SOCS2300</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/SOCS2300">Ethics for the Social Sciences</a></li><li><strong>SOCS2400</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/SOCS2400">Surveying the Social: Applied Quantitative Research</a></li><li><strong>THHE2002</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/THHE2002">Tourism and Intercultural Encounters</a></li><li><em>9 additional courses are part of "Cultures and Citizenship" but not offered in the current academic year, please review the <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/program/2026/11368">handbook</a>.</em></li></ul><p>These are courses currently available in <em>"Cultures and Citizenship"</em>, there may be additional courses available in future terms. For full requirements and course availability always refer to the <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/program/2026/11368">handbook</a>.</p><p><a href="#">Environmental Sustainability</a></p><p><strong>Compulsory </strong>Complete the following compulsory course.</p><ul><li><strong>ENVS3006</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ENVS3006">Surviving the Anthropocene: Sustainability in the 21st Century</a></li></ul><p><strong>Course List </strong>Complete 70 units including a minimum of 30 units at 3000 level.</p><ul><li><strong>ABOR2243</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ABOR2243">Indigenous People and Sustainable Environments</a></li><li><strong>ABOR2245</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ABOR2245">Aboriginal People and Law</a></li><li><strong>ABOR3024</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ABOR3024">Blak Advocacy: Empowerment and Solidarity</a></li><li><strong>ECON3006</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ECON3006">Environmental Economics</a></li><li><strong>ENVS1004</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ENVS1004">Social Development and the Environment</a></li><li><strong>ENVS2002</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ENVS2002">Environmental Legislation and Planning</a></li><li><strong>ENVS2008</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ENVS2008">The Sustainable Society</a></li><li><strong>ENVS3001</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ENVS3001">Integrated Impact Assessment</a></li><li><strong>GEOG2080</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/GEOG2080">Cities and Regions</a></li><li><strong>GEOG3090</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/GEOG3090">Society and Space</a></li><li><strong>IREL2003</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/IREL2003">The International Politics of Climate Change and the Future World</a></li><li><strong>SCIE3600</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/SCIE3600">International Study Experience</a></li><li><strong>SOCA2065</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/SOCA2065">Environment and Society</a></li><li><strong>SOCA3790</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/SOCA3790">Mobilities, Migration and the Environment</a></li><li><strong>THHE3001</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/THHE3001">Managing and Marketing Destinations</a></li><li><em>1 additional courses are part of "Environmental Sustainability" but not offered in the current academic year, please review the <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/program/2026/11368">handbook</a>.</em></li></ul><p>These are courses currently available in <em>"Environmental Sustainability"</em>, there may be additional courses available in future terms. For full requirements and course availability always refer to the <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/program/2026/11368">handbook</a>.</p><p><a href="#">Geopolitics and Economic Development</a></p><p><strong>Compulsory </strong>Complete the following compulsory course.</p><ul><li><strong>IREL3003</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/IREL3003">International Relations in a Changing World Order</a></li></ul><p><strong>Course List </strong>Students must complete 70 units from the following course list, including 30 units of ECON courses and a minimum of 30 units at 3000 level.</p><ul><li><strong>ECON2002</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ECON2002">Intermediate Macroeconomics</a></li><li><strong>ECON2112</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ECON2112"> Health Economics and Finance</a></li><li><strong>ECON3009</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ECON3009">Urban and Regional Economics</a></li><li><strong>ECON3111</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ECON3111">Behavioural Economics</a></li><li><strong>ECON3112</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ECON3112">International Economics and Payment Infrastructure</a></li><li><strong>GEOG1020</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/GEOG1020">Introduction to Human Geography</a></li><li><strong>IREL2001</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/IREL2001">International Policy, Politics and the Economy</a></li><li><strong>IREL2002</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/IREL2002">Business and the Politics of Globalisation</a></li><li><strong>IREL2003</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/IREL2003">The International Politics of Climate Change and the Future World</a></li><li><strong>IREL2004</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/IREL2004">Diplomacy, Trade and Politics</a></li><li><strong>IREL3001</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/IREL3001">Public Diplomacy and Social Media</a></li><li><strong>IREL3002</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/IREL3002">Global Data Governance for Just and Sustainable Futures</a></li><li><strong>IREL3004</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/IREL3004">Indigenising International Politics, Policy and the Economy</a></li><li><strong>THHE3002</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/THHE3002">Strategy for Tourism Futures and Innovation</a></li><li><em>1 additional courses are part of "Geopolitics and Economic Development" but not offered in the current academic year, please review the <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/program/2026/11368">handbook</a>.</em></li></ul><p>These are courses currently available in <em>"Geopolitics and Economic Development"</em>, there may be additional courses available in future terms. For full requirements and course availability always refer to the <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/program/2026/11368">handbook</a>.</p><p><a href="#">Urban and Regional Development</a></p><p><strong>Compulsory </strong>Complete the following compulsory course.</p><ul><li><strong>GEOG3090</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/GEOG3090">Society and Space</a></li></ul><p><strong>Course List </strong>Complete 70 units including a minimum of 30 units at 3000 level.</p><ul><li><strong>ABOR2244</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ABOR2244">Sovereignty and Reconciliation</a></li><li><strong>ABOR2245</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ABOR2245">Aboriginal People and Law</a></li><li><strong>ABOR3024</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ABOR3024">Blak Advocacy: Empowerment and Solidarity</a></li><li><strong>ECON3006</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ECON3006">Environmental Economics</a></li><li><strong>ECON3009</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ECON3009">Urban and Regional Economics</a></li><li><strong>ENVS2002</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ENVS2002">Environmental Legislation and Planning</a></li><li><strong>ENVS2008</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ENVS2008">The Sustainable Society</a></li><li><strong>ENVS3001</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ENVS3001">Integrated Impact Assessment</a></li><li><strong>ENVS3006</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ENVS3006">Surviving the Anthropocene: Sustainability in the 21st Century</a></li><li><strong>GEOG1020</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/GEOG1020">Introduction to Human Geography</a></li><li><strong>GEOG2080</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/GEOG2080">Cities and Regions</a></li><li><strong>GEOS2161</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/GEOS2161">Spatial Science</a></li><li><strong>GEOS3250</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/GEOS3250">Advanced Spatial Science</a></li><li><strong>HUSE3006</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/HUSE3006">Social Policy and Planning in Regional Contexts</a></li><li><strong>SCIE3600</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/SCIE3600">International Study Experience</a></li><li><strong>SOCA2065</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/SOCA2065">Environment and Society</a></li><li><strong>SOCS2400</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/SOCS2400">Surveying the Social: Applied Quantitative Research</a></li><li><strong>THHE3002</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/THHE3002">Strategy for Tourism Futures and Innovation</a></li><li><em>2 additional courses are part of "Urban and Regional Development" but not offered in the current academic year, please review the <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/program/2026/11368">handbook</a>.</em></li></ul><p>These are courses currently available in <em>"Urban and Regional Development"</em>, there may be additional courses available in future terms. For full requirements and course availability always refer to the <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/program/2026/11368">handbook</a>.</p>Electives<p><a href="#">Electives</a></p><p><strong>Complete 40 units of electives to fulfil the requirements of the program. Electives can be used to extend and complement your core studies with more courses in the same field of study, or from areas that might be of interest to you. Electives can be chosen from all courses available at the University that do not have any other conditions (such as a course requisite) applied to them.</strong></p><p>For full requirements and course availability always refer to the <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/program/2026/11368">handbook</a>.</p> 2026 Program Handbook <p>The courses shown above are a guide, and don''t reflect the exact year-by-year structure of the program, you should always refer to the program handbook for details of the complete program structure, course requirements and availability.</p> <p><a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/program/2026/11368">View program handbook</a></p> My Degree Planner <p>Use our degree planner to see what your study journey through the <strong>Bachelor of Development Studies</strong> might look like.</p> <p> <a href="https://degreeplanner.newcastle.edu.au/students/r/pathways?program=11368&amp;region_status=domestic&amp;campus=CALLA&amp;academic_level=UGRD">Prospective student planner</a> <a href="https://askuon.newcastle.edu.au/app/answers/detail/a_id/2438">Current student planner</a> </p> <p> <a href="#student-experience">Student experience</a> <a href="#student-showcase">Student showcase</a><a href="#how-to-apply">How to apply</a> </p> Student experience Meet Kirra, Bachelor of Development Studies student 1:41 <p>Meet University of Newcastle student, Kirra and find out why she chose to study a Bachelor of Development Studies. From international travel opportunities to industry work experience, this degree is preparing Kirra for the real world.</p> Career-ready placements <p>Every student will complete a 140-hour placement with relevant industry partners in government, not-for-profit organisations, or private businesses.</p><p>Students may also undertake international placement, available as an option in every Development Studies major.</p><p>Fieldwork, including on Country with Traditional Custodians, provides the chance to develop research skills.</p> <p> <a href="/current-students/career-ready-placements">Learn more</a> </p> Studying at Newcastle campus <a href="https://www.newcastle.edu.au/campus-life/newcastle/living-in-newcastle"> Living in Newcastle <p>Nestled on Australia''s spectacular east coast and surrounded by some of the state''s most popular destinations, Newcastle is an all-round wonderful choice as a safe and friendly place to live.</p> <p>Read more</p> </a> <a href="https://www.newcastle.edu.au/campus-life"> Campus Life <p>Our campuses are welcoming, inspiring, and collaborative places for students, staff, and the community. Explore our beautiful campuses and discover what it’s like to study at the University of Newcastle.</p> <p>Read more</p> </a> <a href="https://www.newcastle.edu.au/scholarships"> Scholarships <p>Our scholarships recognise achievement and support enthusiastic and dedicated students. If you are a current student, or planning to study with us, we encourage you to apply for all scholarships you meet the criteria for.</p> <p>Read more</p> </a> <a href="https://www.newcastle.edu.au/campus-life/accommodation"> Accommodation <p>Living on campus is an experience unlike any other. Whether you''re from across Australia or around the globe, your home on campus is where you''ll form lifelong friendships and harness your potential in a supportive and diverse community.</p> <p>Read more</p> </a> Student showcase Student Highlights <a href="https://www.newcastle.edu.au/highlights/student/2023/jenna-hawes"> <p>Student</p> Policy and Evaluation; Jenna''s experience <p>Jenna secured a graduate position before finishing her studies and has been working towards her goals ever since.</p> <p>Read more</p> </a><a href="https://www.newcastle.edu.au/highlights/student/2023/caitlin-ganzer"> <p>Student</p> Understanding the potential of Development Studies <p>Experiencing the wide variety of directions the Bachelor of Development Studies can take you, Caitlin changed her original plans and has landed in her dream career. Find out more about her journey here.</p> <p>Read more</p> </a><a href="https://www.newcastle.edu.au/highlights/student/2023/adam-kennedy"> <p>Student</p> Taking Urban Planning to new heights <p>After having an interest in urban planning, Adam realised this could be a career path worth achieving. Find out how more about Adam''s story here.</p> <p>Read more</p> </a><a href="https://www.newcastle.edu.au/highlights/student/2023/alice-jarrett"> <p>Student</p> Challenging thinking and communication methods <p>Since working within the WaterNSW team, Alice has implemented her skills from studying to understand the changes and approaches to community development. Find out more about her story here.</p> <p>Read more</p> </a><a href="https://www.newcastle.edu.au/highlights/student/2023/elizabeth-scroppe"> <p>Student</p> Advocating for the disadvantaged <p>When Elizabeth first started her degree, her focus was on policy and procedure. However since undertaking work placement, her career has been redirected. Find out more about her career change and what led to this here.</p> <p>Read more</p> </a><a href="https://www.newcastle.edu.au/highlights/student/2023/connor-ashleigh"> <p>Student</p> The Visual Appeal of Development Studies <p>Conor took his understanding of environmental sustainability from the Bachelor of Development Studies and implemented this across his independent contractor visual work. Find out how he was able to use this to his advantage.</p> <p>Read more</p> </a> How to apply <p>Domestic</p> <p>International</p> Apply as a domestic student <p><strong>You are a domestic student if you are:</strong></p> <ul> <li>An Australian or New Zealand citizen (including dual citizens)</li> <li>A permanent resident of Australia</li> <li>A holder of a permanent Australian visa.</li> </ul> <p><a href="#apply-now">Apply now</a><p><a href="https://newcastle.uac.edu.au/apply"><strong>Apply Direct</strong></a></p><p><a href="https://uac.edu.au/course-search/search/undergraduate/course/482550"><strong>Apply via UAC</strong></a></p> <a href="https://www.newcastle.edu.au/study/undergraduate/admissions-and-entry">How to get into uni</a></p> Apply as an international student Entry requirements — Senior secondary school qualification <p>Admission to our undergraduate degree programs usually requires successful completion of a senior secondary school qualification similar to the <a href="//www.boardofstudies.nsw.edu.au/hsc/">Higher School Certificate (HSC)</a>, the highest educational award in New South Wales schools.</p> <ul> <li><a href="https://www.newcastle.edu.au/study/international/study-with-us/what-can-i-study">More about entrance marks</a></li> </ul> English proficiency requirements <p>Applicants who do not speak English as a first language must demonstrate that they meet the University''s English proficiency requirement. Further information regarding English language proficiency requirements can be found at the <a href="https://policies.newcastle.edu.au/document/view-current.php?id=165">English Language Proficiency for Admission Policy</a>.</p> <ul> <li><strong>IELTS overall minimum</strong> - 6.0</li> <li><strong>IELTS section minimum</strong> - 6.0</li> <li><a href="https://www.newcastle.edu.au/study/pathways/english-language-pathways">About English Language Pathways</a></li> </ul> Apply to the University via our online application portal. <p>In the application portal, you can enter the program you wish to study and upload relevant documents needed as part of your application. It is recommended you submit your application a minimum of 12 weeks before your intended semester of trimester start date.</p> <ul> <li><a href="https://www.newcastle.edu.au/degrees/how-to-apply/what-you-will-need">What information you will need</a></li> </ul> <p>If you are applying through an education agent, they can also apply through the application portal. Ensure your agent is contracted to the University to avoid delays with your application.</p> <ul> <li><a href="https://www.newcastle.edu.au/study/international/the-university-in-your-country">Find an education agent in your country</a></li> </ul> <p>Once you have started your application you will be provided with a reference number. You or your education agent will be able to track your application as it progresses.</p> <p><a href="https://www.newcastle.edu.au/study/international/admissions?ctx_prg=11368">Apply online</a></p> Similar degrees <a href="bachelor-of-social-science">Bachelor of Social Science<ul><li><strong>Selection Rank:</strong> 63.00 (Newcastle)</li><li><strong>Indicative Fee1:</strong> AUD 37,200</li><li><strong>Full-time duration:</strong> 3 years</li><li><strong>Part-time duration:</strong> up to 8 years</li><li><strong>Locations:</strong> Newcastle, Gosford</li></ul><p>Learn more</p></a> <a href="bachelor-of-arts">Bachelor of Arts<ul><li><strong>Selection Rank:</strong> 63.00 (Newcastle)</li><li><strong>Indicative Fee1:</strong> AUD 36,450</li><li><strong>Full-time duration:</strong> 3 years</li><li><strong>Part-time duration:</strong> up to 8 years</li><li><strong>Locations:</strong> Newcastle, Online</li></ul><p>Learn more</p></a> <a href="bachelor-of-psychological-science">Bachelor of Psychological Science<ul><li><strong>Selection Rank:</strong> 67.00 (Newcastle)</li><li><strong>Indicative Fee1:</strong> AUD 45,070</li><li><strong>Full-time duration:</strong> 3 years</li><li><strong>Part-time duration:</strong> up to 8 years</li><li><strong>Locations:</strong> Newcastle, Gosford</li></ul><p>Learn more</p></a> <p>1 Indicative annual fees are based on a full year full time load (80 units). <a href="https://www.newcastle.edu.au/current-students/study-essentials/fees-scholarships/types-of-tuition-fees">Learn more.</a></p> <p> <a href="combined">View combined degrees</a> <a href="/study/humanities-and-social-sciences">Explore study area</a> </p> Looking for more information? <p> Ask a question </p> <p> <a href="https://askuon.custhelp.com/">Find your answer</a> </p> <p> Call 1300 275 866 </p> <p> <a href="tel:1300275866">Call us now</a> </p> <p> Sign up for updates </p> <p> <a href="https://www.newcastle.edu.au/study/stay-connected">Sign up now</a> </p> Looking for more information? <p> Ask a question </p> <p> <a href="https://apply.newcastle.edu.au/international-enquirySPApp/">Find your answer</a> </p> <p> Call +61 2 4913 8300 </p> <p> <a href="tel:61249138300">Call us now</a> </p> <p> Sign up for updates </p> <p> <a href="https://www.newcastle.edu.au/study/stay-connected#international">Sign up now</a> </p> <h4>Career-ready placements</h4><p>Every student will complete a 140-hour placement with relevant industry partners in government, not-for-profit organisations, or private businesses.</p><p>Students may also undertake international placement, available as an option in every Development Studies major.</p><p>Fieldwork, including on Country with Traditional Custodians, provides the chance to develop research skills.</p><p> <a href="/current-students/career-ready-placements">Learn more</a> </p>',
