UPDATE courses SET
    course_description = '<h4>Degree overview</h4><p>Understand the world around us and how it works</p><p>Science is about asking new questions and then answering them. Scientists are right at the heart of understanding the way our universe and world works, building new technologies, managing our environment, and improving our health. The skills and networks you develop while studying will make you a skilled and employable graduate ready to contribute solutions to the complex challenges we face.</p> <p>Join a high-achieving cohort. As an advanced science student you will have the opportunity to join research teams across the institution and learn from leading scientists. You’ll begin the process of establishing the professional networks that will be critical throughout your career through authentic opportunities to work in and with industry while you study.</p><p>Tailor the program to suit your interests. You’ll be able to choose from 10 diverse majors. There’s flexibility to pick from a wide range of electives, or pursue a double major.</p><p><strong>Concurrent Enrolment</strong></p><p>Bachelor of Science (Advanced) students are encouraged to consider concurrent enrolment in the <a href="https://www.newcastle.edu.au/degrees/bachelor-of-applied-data-science">Bachelor of Applied Data Science program.</a> <br/><br/>A career in science is increasingly data-driven. From climate modelling to biomedical research, the ability to manage, interpret, and visualise data is essential. By combining a science major with the Bachelor of Applied Data Science, students gain advanced data analysis and visualisation skills, confidence in programming and database management, and tools for evidence-based decision-making.</p><p>Complete both programs in only one extra year of study and graduate with two degrees, increasing your in-demand skills and employability in broad and varied areas such as environmental and climate science, pharmaceuticals and biomedical research, geoscience, physics and engineering, and science communication and policy.</p> <p> <strong> World top 200 ? </strong> for Environmental Sciences </p> <p> <strong> 88.4% of students ? </strong> were happy with facilities and resources </p> <h4>Career opportunities</h4><p>Most of the critical issues that face our society today require science to help find the right solutions for our current and future generations. Because the Bachelor of Science emphasises problem-solving, teamwork and critical thinking, graduates are sought after across a range of sectors.</p> <p>Use this degree as a steppingstone into the health and science fields to achieve your career goals.</p><p><strong>Professional outcomes:</strong></p><ul><li>Animal biologist</li><li>Chemist</li><li>Conservationist/ecologist</li><li>Data scientist or financial analyst</li><li>Geologist</li><li>Government or defence professional</li><li>Mathematician</li><li>Medical physicist</li><li>Meteorologist</li><li>Neuroscientist</li><li>Oceanographer</li><li>Research physicist</li><li>Statistician</li></ul> <p>88.5% employed within four months of graduating.</p> <p>Graduate Outcomes Survey 2022-2024 - Undergraduate Science &amp; Mathematics</p> <h4>Course overview</h4><p>Tailor your degree to your interests with 10 diverse majors and flexible study pathways, allowing you to focus on areas that inspire you.</p><p>You''ll develop highly sought-after and transferable problem-solving, communication and independent thinking skills.</p><p>You''ll study core courses that give you the edge in the following areas:</p><ul><li>scientific thinking</li><li>communicating as a scientist</li><li>lab and fieldwork skills</li><li>problem solving</li><li>project planning</li><li>project implementation</li></ul> <p>Core</p><p>Majors</p><p>Electives</p> <p><a href="#">Core</a></p><p><strong>Complete the following core courses to fulfil the requirements of this program.</strong></p><ul><li><strong>INFT1004</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/INFT1004">Introduction to Programming</a></li><li><strong>SCIE1102</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/SCIE1102">Advanced Multidisciplinary Laboratories</a></li><li><strong>SCIE3002</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/SCIE3002">WIL for the Sciences</a></li></ul><p>These are courses currently available in <em>"Core"</em>, there may be additional courses available in future terms. For full requirements and course availability always refer to the <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/program/2026/40166">handbook</a>.</p><p><a href="#">Directed Course List</a></p><p><strong>Complete 10 units from the courses below. If completing the Physics Major choose MATH2310</strong></p><ul><li><strong>MATH2310</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/MATH2310">Calculus of Science and Engineering</a></li><li><strong>SCIE2510</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/SCIE2510">Research Project</a></li></ul><p>These are courses currently available in <em>"Directed Course List"</em>, there may be additional courses available in future terms. For full requirements and course availability always refer to the <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/program/2026/40166">handbook</a>.</p><p><a href="#">Mathematics Course List</a></p><p><strong>Complete 10 units from the following Directed Courses. Which course you should complete will depend on your previous mathematical background, please refer to each course handbook for more information. MATH1002 or higher is required for a Chemistry Major. MATH1110 is required for Physics or Mathematics Majors.</strong></p><ul><li><strong>MATH1001</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/MATH1001">Preparatory Studies in Mathematics</a></li><li><strong>MATH1002</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/MATH1002">Foundational Studies in Mathematics</a></li><li><strong>MATH1110</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/MATH1110">Mathematics for Engineering, Science and Technology 1</a></li></ul><p>These are courses currently available in <em>"Mathematics Course List"</em>, there may be additional courses available in future terms. For full requirements and course availability always refer to the <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/program/2026/40166">handbook</a>.</p><p><a href="#">Statistics Course List</a></p><p><strong>Complete 10 units from the following course list. The course you choose should align with your prior knowledge of statistics and mathematics. For detailed information about each course, please refer to the course handbook. If you are pursuing a major in Physics or Mathematics, it is recommended to complete STAT1300.</strong></p><ul><li><strong>STAT1070</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/STAT1070">Statistics for the Sciences</a></li><li><strong>STAT1300</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/STAT1300">Fundamentals of Statistics</a></li></ul><p>These are courses currently available in <em>"Statistics Course List"</em>, there may be additional courses available in future terms. For full requirements and course availability always refer to the <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/program/2026/40166">handbook</a>.</p><p><a href="#">Science Course List (for students who complete single major only)</a></p><p><strong>Complete either a second major, or 80 units from the Science course list (all major courses + SCIE), or 50 units if you are doing a Physics major. Students must complete at least 20 units from 2000 level courses and at least 20 units from 3000 level courses.</strong></p><ul><li><strong>BIOL1001</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/BIOL1001">Molecules, Cells and Organisms</a></li><li><strong>BIOL1002</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/BIOL1002">Organisms to Ecosystems</a></li><li><strong>BIOL2001</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/BIOL2001">Molecular Laboratory Skills for Biological Sciences</a></li><li><strong>BIOL2002</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/BIOL2002">Laboratory Skills in Biological Systems</a></li><li><strong>BIOL2090</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/BIOL2090">Microbial Biology</a></li><li><strong>BIOL2220</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/BIOL2220">Plant Adaptation to Climate Change</a></li><li><strong>BIOL3001</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/BIOL3001">Advanced Laboratory Skills in Biological Sciences</a></li><li><strong>BIOL3020</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/BIOL3020">Animal Physiology, Reproduction and Development</a></li><li><strong>BIOL3090</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/BIOL3090">Molecular Biology</a></li><li><strong>BIOL3100</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/BIOL3100">Microbiology</a></li><li><strong>CHEM1010</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/CHEM1010">Introductory Chemistry I</a></li><li><strong>CHEM1020</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/CHEM1020">Introductory Chemistry II</a></li><li><strong>CHEM2110</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/CHEM2110">Applied Analytical Chemistry</a></li><li><strong>CHEM2210</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/CHEM2210">Materials Chemistry</a></li><li><strong>CHEM2310</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/CHEM2310">Organic Chemistry</a></li><li><strong>CHEM2410</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/CHEM2410">Physical Chemistry</a></li><li><strong>CHEM3110</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/CHEM3110">Instrumental Chemical Analysis</a></li><li><strong>CHEM3210</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/CHEM3210">Functional Materials</a></li><li><strong>CHEM3310</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/CHEM3310">Molecular Organic Synthesis</a></li><li><strong>CHEM3410</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/CHEM3410">Energy and Materials</a></li><li><strong>CHEM3550</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/CHEM3550">Medicinal and Biological Chemistry</a></li><li><strong>CHEM3580</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/CHEM3580">Colloids, Interfaces and Soft Matter</a></li><li><strong>CMNS1031</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/CMNS1031">Communication Skills</a></li><li><strong>CMNS1090</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/CMNS1090">Introduction to Professional Writing</a></li><li><strong>CMNS1100</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/CMNS1100">Media, Culture and Narrative</a></li><li><strong>CMNS2140</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/CMNS2140">Principles of Public Relations</a></li><li><strong>CMNS2320</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/CMNS2320">News Media</a></li><li><strong>CMNS3150</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/CMNS3150">Podcasting</a></li><li><strong>CMNS3333</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/CMNS3333">Multiplatform Journalism</a></li><li><strong>CMNS3470</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/CMNS3470">Data Visualisation in Journalism</a></li><li><strong>CMNS3520</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/CMNS3520">Political Communication</a></li><li><strong>CMNS3666</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/CMNS3666">Contemporary Journalism</a></li><li><strong>CMNS3737</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/CMNS3737">Social Media Listening and Monitoring</a></li><li><strong>DESN2060</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/DESN2060">Scientific Illustration</a></li><li><strong>DESN2211</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/DESN2211">Visual Narrative</a></li><li><strong>ENVS1001</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ENVS1001">Environmental Science Concepts and Methods</a></li><li><strong>ENVS1003</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ENVS1003">Environmental Values and Ethics</a></li><li><strong>ENVS1004</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ENVS1004">Social Development and the Environment</a></li><li><strong>ENVS2002</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ENVS2002">Environmental Legislation and Planning</a></li><li><strong>ENVS2004</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ENVS2004">Ecology</a></li><li><strong>ENVS2005</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ENVS2005">Management of Australian Flora</a></li><li><strong>ENVS2006</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ENVS2006">Ecology and Management of Wildlife</a></li><li><strong>ENVS2008</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ENVS2008">The Sustainable Society</a></li><li><strong>ENVS2009</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ENVS2009">Catchment and Water Resource Management</a></li><li><strong>ENVS2320</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ENVS2320">Experimental Design and Analysis in Ecology</a></li><li><strong>ENVS3001</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ENVS3001">Integrated Impact Assessment</a></li><li><strong>ENVS3003</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026/ENVS3003">Conservation Biology</a></li><li><strong>ENVS3004</strong> <a href="https://handbook.newcastle.edu.au/course/2026